    summary: "Read the workflow header as the rule that decides when and where CI exists.",
    summaryUk: "Читайте workflow header як правило, що вирішує, коли і де існує CI.",
    path: ".github/workflows/desktop-ui-tests.yml",
    codeLanguage: "yaml",
    code: "name: Desktop UI tests\n\non:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [main]\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: desktop-ui-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true\n\njobs:\n  desktop-tests:\n    name: Notepad UI tests + screenshots + Allure\n    runs-on: windows-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 20",
    what: "GitHub creates a workflow run for manual dispatch, pushes to `main`, and pull requests targeting `main`. The job gets read-only repository content permission, shares a concurrency group keyed by Git ref so a newer run can cancel an obsolete in-progress run for the same ref, and is scheduled onto a temporary GitHub-hosted `windows-latest` runner with a 20-minute job limit.",
    whatUk: "GitHub створює workflow run для manual dispatch, pushes у `main` та pull requests до `main`. Job отримує read-only repository content permission, має concurrency group за Git ref, тому newer run може cancel obsolete in-progress run того самого ref, і scheduler відправляє його на temporary GitHub-hosted `windows-latest` runner з 20-minute job limit.",
    why: "This header defines the CI contract before any command executes: events, permissions, cancellation behavior, operating system and maximum duration.",
    whyUk: "Цей header визначає CI contract до виконання будь-якої command: events, permissions, cancellation behavior, operating system та maximum duration.",
